'''La Cruïlla''' is a city in the [[Cumará (comarca)|Cumará comarca]], [[Estret Province]], [[Balboa]]. With a population of 351,643 in 2026, it is the third-largest municipality in Cumará after [[Varakí]] and [[Enllaç]]. La Cruïlla occupies the central portion of the comarca, between the [[Sant Cristòfor (comarca)|Sant Cristòfor]] metropolitan area to the north and [[Varakí]] to the south, and functions as a suburban commuter municipality linking [[Sant Cristòfor]] and the [[International City of Areza|International City of Areza]].

The city's name means "the crossroads" in Volisanian, referring to the junction of the Zong-era central railway line — which ran north–south from Sant Cristòfor toward the canal zone — and the historic east–west road that crossed it. The construction of [[Metro de Sant Cristòfor|Metro Line 8]] and [[Metro de Sant Cristòfor|Metro Line 9]] in the post-reunification period reinforced this identity, making La Cruïlla one of the principal transit interchanges in the southern Estret corridor.

== Geography ==

La Cruïlla occupies 181.0 km² on the flat lowlands of the southern Estret corridor, south of the [[Sant Cristòfor (comarca)|Sant Cristòfor]] metropolitan area. The municipality borders the Sant Cristòfor comarca to the north, [[Varakí]] to the south, [[Els Camps]] and [[Brises]] to the east, and [[Belljardí]] and [[Enllaç]] to the west. The terrain is generally flat, with a gradual rise toward the eastern portion of the municipality where the land begins to climb toward the foothills of the [[Serralada d'Estret]].

The Areza Subdivision — the main rail corridor between Sant Cristòfor and the [[International City of Areza|International City of Areza]] — runs roughly north–south through the central portion of the municipality, following the alignment of the original Zong-era central railway line. [[Metro de Sant Cristòfor|Metro Line 9]] runs parallel to the east of the rail corridor, through the canal corridor alignment, while [[Metro de Sant Cristòfor|Metro Line 8]] terminates in the western portion of the city. The [[Expressway 68 (Balboa)|Expressway 68]] crosses the municipality from west to east, intersecting the north–south transport corridors and creating the junction that gives the city its name.

=== Climate ===

La Cruïlla has a {{w|tropical monsoon climate}} (Köppen ''Am''), consistent with the southern Estret lowlands. Temperatures are warm year-round, with average daily highs of 30–32 °C and lows of 22–24 °C. The wet season runs from May to November, with the heaviest rainfall from June to August. The flat terrain and inland position — removed from the lakefront moderation that benefits Varakí — can produce slightly higher daytime temperatures and more pronounced humidity during the wet season.

== History ==

=== Zong Balboa period (1899–1950) ===

{{main|Zong Balboa}}

La Cruïlla originated as a small junction settlement where the Zong-era central railway line, extended southward from [[Sant Cristòfor]] toward the canal zone, crossed the historic east–west road that connected the Shendan coast to the interior. The crossing gave the settlement its name — ''la cruïlla'', Volisanian for "the crossroads." A small rail stop was established at the junction to serve the surrounding agricultural land and the few families who lived near the crossing. The settlement remained small throughout the Zong period, consisting of a railway halt, a cluster of workers' housing, and a few commercial buildings serving the local farming community.

The [[Great Adonian War]] (1935–1947) largely bypassed La Cruïlla, which was too small and too far from the fighting to be directly affected. The railway line was maintained for military logistics, and the rail stop remained operational throughout the war.

=== AC mandate and Areza construction boom (1950–1973) ===

{{main|International City of Areza|Treaty of Areza}}

The [[Adonian Community]] mandate period transformed La Cruïlla's fortunes, as it did for the rest of the southern Estret corridor. The construction of the [[International City of Areza]] — which began in earnest in the late 1950s and continued through the 1960s — drew thousands of workers to the area. La Cruïlla, situated on the main rail line between Sant Cristòfor and the Areza construction site, became a natural residential base for the construction workforce. The population swelled from a few hundred to several thousand within a decade.

Informal settlements and worker housing spread outward from the original crossroads junction, and the municipal government — established in 1961 when the post-reunification territorial reorganisation separated Cumará from Sant Cristòfor — struggled to keep pace with the growth. The [[Treaty of Areza]] (1973), which transferred the canal zone to Balboan sovereignty and completed the reunification of Estret Province, further accelerated development by opening the corridor to private investment and commuter rail services.

=== Post-reunification and metro expansion (1973–present) ===

The post-reunification period brought sustained growth to La Cruïlla, driven by its position at the junction of multiple transport corridors. The establishment of CristoCarril Line C as a formal commuter rail service, with stations in La Cruïlla along the original Zong-era railway alignment, connected the municipality directly to the Sant Cristòfor rail network and to Varakí and the southern corridor.

The extension of [[Metro de Sant Cristòfor|Metro Line 8]] from Sant Cristòfor to its terminus in western La Cruïlla provided the municipality with its first rapid transit connection. The opening of [[Metro de Sant Cristòfor|Metro Line 9]] in 2003, which runs north–south through the eastern portion of the municipality along the canal corridor alignment, added a second metro line and reinforced La Cruïlla's role as a transit interchange. The construction of [[Expressway 68 (Balboa)|Expressway 68]] through the municipality created a modern east–west road connection that intersected the north–south rail and metro corridors, updating the historic crossroads for the automobile era.

The population grew steadily through the 1980s and 1990s, with apartment blocks and townhouse developments spreading outward from the transport corridors. By the 2020s, La Cruïlla had established itself as a mid-density commuter suburb with a diverse population drawn by its transit access to both Sant Cristòfor and Areza.

== Government ==

La Cruïlla is governed by an elected {{w|mayor}} and a municipal council. The mayor serves four-year terms and has executive authority over municipal administration, planning, and local services. The current mayor is [[Antoni Riera i Vives]] of the [[Liberal Party of Balboa]] (PLB), elected in 2024. Riera i Vives, a former municipal transport planner, campaigned on transit integration, road improvements, and the regulation of apartment development along the metro corridors. The PLB's strength in La Cruïlla reflects the city's middle-class commuter population and the growing professional class attracted by the dual metro access.

== Demographics ==

La Cruïlla's population of 351,643 (2026) makes it the third-largest municipality in the Cumará comarca. The population is predominantly commuter-oriented, with a large proportion of working-age residents who commute daily to Sant Cristòfor or Areza via metro or commuter rail.

The ethnic composition reflects the comarca's suburban character and the successive waves of migration that shaped the city. [[Black Balboan people|Black Balboans]] constitute the largest group, concentrated in the older neighbourhoods near the original crossroads junction and the northern housing estates. [[Mixed Balboan people|Mixed Balboans]] are distributed throughout the municipality, particularly in the apartment developments along the metro corridors. [[White Balboan people|White Balboans]] are present in the newer western and eastern developments, where transit-oriented housing has attracted a growing professional class. The [[Fosian Balboan people|Fosian]] community, including both [[Sinchewese Balboan people|Sinchew]] and [[Zong Balboan people|Zong]] Balboans, is concentrated along the rail corridor and in the commercial districts near the original junction, reflecting the area's Zong-era railway heritage.

The city's population is younger than the Estret average, reflecting the influx of working-age commuters, and the household size is slightly smaller than the national average, consistent with the proportion of apartment-dwelling single professionals and young families.

== Economy ==

La Cruïlla's economy is based on commuting, residential services, retail, and local commerce. The city functions primarily as a bedroom community for both Sant Cristòfor and Areza, with a large proportion of the working population commuting daily by metro or commuter rail. The dual metro access — Line 8 to Sant Cristòfor and Line 9 to both Sant Cristòfor and Areza — gives residents a choice of destinations unmatched by most other municipalities in the comarca.

The commercial district around the original crossroads junction — where the rail corridor meets Expressway 68 — is the city's principal retail and service centre, with shops, restaurants, and small businesses serving both the local population and pass-through traffic. The areas around the Metro Line 8 terminus and the Metro Line 9 stations have developed as secondary commercial nodes, with transit-oriented retail and service businesses.

Local industry is limited, consisting primarily of small-scale logistics, construction-related businesses, and light manufacturing clustered along the rail corridor and the expressway. The municipality's agricultural fringe, in the eastern and southern areas where the terrain rises toward the Serralada, supports small-scale farming and market gardens.

== Transport ==

La Cruïlla's transport network is built around its crossroads position, with multiple rail and metro corridors converging in the municipality. The system integrates with the Sant Cristòfor metro and the national rail network.

=== Commuter rail ===

{{main|CristoCarril}}

CristoCarril Line C, operated by [[Ferrocarrils de l'Istme]], runs north–south through the central portion of La Cruïlla, following the alignment of the original Zong-era central railway line. Line C connects La Cruïlla to the Sant Cristòfor rail network to the north and to [[Varakí]] and the southern corridor toward [[Areza]] to the south. The line has three stations within La Cruïlla:

