The high-speed rail line between Sant Cristòfor and Areza runs on the same corridor but does not stop in La Cruïlla; the nearest HSR stop is at Varakí.

=== Metro ===

{{main|Metro de Sant Cristòfor}}

[[Metro de Sant Cristòfor|Metro Line 8]] terminates in western La Cruïlla, providing a direct rapid transit connection to Sant Cristòfor. The line was extended to La Cruïlla during the post-reunification metro expansion, and its terminus station serves as the primary transport node for the western neighbourhoods. The line carries commuters northward to the Sant Cristòfor metro network, with a journey time of approximately 25 minutes to the central city.

[[Metro de Sant Cristòfor|Metro Line 9]], opened in 2003, runs north–south through the eastern portion of La Cruïlla, following the canal corridor alignment between Sant Cristòfor and Areza. The line has three stations within La Cruïlla, serving the eastern residential districts and the transit-oriented developments along the corridor. Line 9 connects La Cruïlla to both Sant Cristòfor to the north and the [[Areza Metro]] to the south, giving residents direct rapid transit access to both destinations.

=== Roads ===

[[Expressway 68 (Balboa)|Expressway 68]] crosses La Cruïlla from west to east, intersecting the north–south rail and metro corridors. The expressway provides road connections to the broader Estret Province road network, linking La Cruïlla to the Shendan coast to the west and the interior toward the Serralada d'Estret to the east. Local roads connect the residential neighbourhoods to the expressway interchanges and the neighbouring municipalities.

=== Bus ===

The ''Autobus de La Cruïlla'' (AdLC) is the municipal bus network operated by the city government. The network consists of six lines, all of which originate from the La Cruïlla transit centre — the integrated transport interchange located at the Autopista 68/La Cruïlla CristoCarril Line C station in Zona 1. From the transit centre, the lines radiate outward to serve the residential neighbourhoods, the metro stations, and the lower-density areas between the transport corridors. The AdLC also provides feeder connections to the Metro Line 8 terminus in the west, the Metro Line 9 stations in the east, and the CristoCarril Line C stations at Torre Fàcil and Carretera Udi. Several lines extend beyond the municipal boundary to connect La Cruïlla with the surrounding municipalities in the Cumará comarca, including [[Enllaç]], [[Belljardí]], [[Els Camps]], and [[Varakí]].

== Neighbourhoods ==

La Cruïlla is divided into five municipal zones, each reflecting a different period and pattern of the city's growth:

=== Zona 1 — Central ===

'''Zona 1''' is the oldest part of the city, centred on the original crossroads junction where the Zong-era central railway line met the historic east–west road. The street pattern retains the grid layout of the original junction settlement, with the rail corridor running through the centre and the old road — now part of Expressway 68 — crossing it. The housing stock consists of a mix of Zong-era workers' cottages, post-reunification apartment blocks, and newer infill development. Zona 1 is the civic and commercial hub of the municipality, containing the municipal government offices, the main commercial district, and the CristoCarril Line C station at Autopista 68/La Cruïlla, which also serves as the La Cruïlla transit centre — the hub of the municipal bus network. The area is the most ethnically diverse part of the city, with a large [[Black Balboan people|Black Balboan]] population and a significant [[Fosian Balboan people|Fosian]] community dating to the Zong-era railway heritage.

=== Zona 2 — Nord ===

'''Zona 2''' is the northernmost area of the municipality, bordering the [[Sant Cristòfor (comarca)|Sant Cristòfor]] metropolitan area. The zone developed first as a commuter suburb, taking advantage of the proximity to Sant Cristòfor and the rail connection via CristoCarril Line C. The housing stock consists predominantly of mid-rise apartment blocks built in the 1970s and 1980s, with some newer townhouse developments. Zona 2 is the densest part of La Cruïlla, and the CristoCarril Line C station at Torre Fàcil serves as the primary commuter departure point for residents heading to Sant Cristòfor. The population is predominantly working-age commuters.

=== Zona 3 — Occidental ===

'''Zona 3''' is the western portion of the municipality, developed around the [[Metro de Sant Cristòfor|Metro Line 8]] terminus. The area was largely agricultural land until the Line 8 extension in the post-reunification period, after which it became the focus of transit-oriented residential development. The housing stock is newer than in Zonas 1 and 2, with a mix of mid-rise apartment blocks, townhouses, and garden apartments built from the 1990s onward. The Line 8 terminus station serves as the primary transport node for the zone, and the surrounding area has developed a secondary commercial district with retail and service businesses catering to the commuter population.

=== Zona 4 — Oriental ===

'''Zona 4''' is the eastern portion of the municipality, developed along the [[Metro de Sant Cristòfor|Metro Line 9]] corridor. The zone is the newest part of La Cruïlla, with much of its development dating from the 2000s and 2010s following the opening of Line 9 in 2003. The housing stock consists of modern mid-rise apartment blocks and townhouse developments, laid out along the three Line 9 stations that serve the zone. Unlike Zona 2, which commutes predominantly to Sant Cristòfor, residents of Zona 4 have direct metro access to both Sant Cristòfor to the north and the [[International City of Areza|International City of Areza]] to the south, giving the zone a dual-commuter character similar to the eastern neighbourhoods of Varakí. The population is younger and more affluent than in the older zones, drawn by the direct metro access and the newer housing stock.

=== Zona 5 — Sud ===

'''Zona 5''' is the southern portion of the municipality, bordering [[Varakí]]. The zone is lower in density than the northern and central areas, with a mix of detached housing, garden apartments, and small-scale farming along the southern edge. The CristoCarril Line C station at Carretera Udi serves the zone, providing a rail connection northward to Sant Cristòfor and southward to Varakí. The area retains a more suburban and semi-rural character than the rest of La Cruïlla, and the transition toward Varakí's northern neighbourhoods is gradual rather than sharply defined.

== Notable places ==

* '''Cruïlla Junction''' — the original crossroads where the Zong-era central railway line crossed the historic east–west road, now the intersection of the rail corridor and Expressway 68. The junction is the historic core of the city and the source of its name.
* '''La Cruïlla Metro Line 8 terminus''' — the western terminus of Metro Line 8, serving as the primary transport node for Zona 3 and the surrounding residential developments.
* '''Torre Fàcil station''' — the northernmost CristoCarril Line C station in La Cruïlla, serving the dense apartment districts of Zona 2 and acting as the primary commuter departure point toward Sant Cristòfor.
* '''Autopista 68/La Cruïlla station''' — the central CristoCarril Line C station, located at the original crossroads junction in Zona 1. The station complex includes the La Cruïlla transit centre, the hub of the municipal ''Autobus de La Cruïlla'' (AdLC) bus network, where all six AdLC lines originate.
* '''Carretera Udi station''' — the southernmost CristoCarril Line C station in La Cruïlla, serving the lower-density residential neighbourhoods of Zona 5 and providing the connection toward Varakí.

